-- title:
--   Divisor table on the resolution of uv=t^e
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring, $t \in W$ and $e$ a natural number, and write $\mathrm{CrossingQuotient}\,W\,s$ for the quotient $W[X_0,X_1]/(X_0X_1 - s)$ and $\mathrm{Res}(t,e) =$ `Resolution t e` for the scheme obtained as the colimit of the diagram `glueDiagram t e` over the index category `GlueIndex e`, whose objects are copies of $\operatorname{Spec}$ of crossing quotients. Let $F : \mathrm{Fin}(e+1) \to \mathrm{Res}(t,e).\mathrm{IdealSheafData}$ be a family of quasi-coherent ideal sheaf data on the resolution, and assume that for every chart index $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,e$ and every $k \in \mathrm{Fin}(e+1)$ the pull-back $(F_k).\mathrm{comap}\,(\iota\,t\,e\,i)$ along the $i$-th chart morphism $\iota\,t\,e\,i$ into the resolution is the ideal sheaf attached, via `ofIdealTop`, to the ideal of global sections of $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{CrossingQuotient}\,W\,t)$ obtained by transporting along the inverse of `Scheme.ΓSpecIso` the ideal $\,(V\,t)$ if $k = i$, the ideal $(U\,t)$ if $k = i+1$, and the unit ideal otherwise, where $U\,s$ and $V\,s$ denote the two distinguished elements of $\mathrm{CrossingQuotient}\,W\,s$. Then three equalities of ideal sheaf data on $\mathrm{Res}(t,e)$ hold: $\prod_k F_k^{\,k}$ is the ideal sheaf generated by the global section obtained by pulling back $U(t^e)$ along `toCrossing t e`; $\prod_k F_k^{\,e-k}$ is the one generated by the pull-back of $V(t^e)$; and $\prod_k F_k$ is the one generated by the pull-back of $t \in W$ along `toSpec t e`, the composite of `toCrossing t e` with $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the structure map $W \to \mathrm{CrossingQuotient}\,W\,(t^e)$, in each case as the `ofIdealTop` ideal sheaf of the span of that single global section.
--
--   This is the divisor table of the toric ($A_{e-1}$) resolution of the crossing $uv = t^e$: the $e+1$ ideal sheaves characterised chartwise by the hypothesis are the strict transforms of the two branches together with the exceptional components, and the three identities record $\operatorname{div}(u) = \sum_k k F_k$, $\operatorname{div}(v) = \sum_k (e-k) F_k$ and $\operatorname{div}(t) = \sum_k F_k$ in terms of products of ideal sheaves. It feeds the description of the special fibre of the resolution from its chart table, used in the study of degenerating curves over a base in which $t$ is a parameter.
